1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,593
It's eleven at night. You're walking back to your car.

2
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:08,706
The street is empty, the lights are behind you, and then you hear something.

3
00:00:08,796 --> 00:00:13,003
A sound. Behind you. In the dark, somewhere you can't quite see.

4
00:00:13,093 --> 00:00:15,812
And you don't run. You don't spin around.

5
00:00:15,852 --> 00:00:20,389
You just walk a little faster, and you tell yourself it was nothing.

6
00:00:20,479 --> 00:00:26,358
That feeling — that exact one, the one in the back of your neck — is older than farming.

7
00:00:26,398 --> 00:00:30,393
Older than cities. Older than every word you've ever spoken.

8
00:00:30,483 --> 00:00:33,213
It's about two hundred thousand years old.

9
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:36,033
And once, it belonged to a man named Renn.

10
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:44,896
Two hundred thousand years ago, near a lake in what is now northern Germany, the sun is going down and Renn has not stopped talking.

11
00:00:44,986 --> 00:00:48,666
He's tall. He's wearing a hide that used to be a horse.

12
00:00:48,706 --> 00:00:54,888
He's carrying a wooden spear that took somebody days to make, and he is explaining — again —

13
00:00:54,928 --> 00:00:56,444
how good he is with it.

14
00:00:56,534 --> 00:00:58,312
Sena has heard this before.

15
00:00:58,352 --> 00:01:03,226
She's in the ochre hide, she's gutting a fish, and she is not looking up.

16
00:01:03,316 --> 00:01:09,466
Tep, who is eight, is looking up. Tep thinks Renn is the greatest hunter who has ever lived.

17
00:01:09,506 --> 00:01:11,417
Tep is wrong, but it's sweet.

18
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:17,317
This is the part nobody puts in the documentaries. Not the spears, not the bones. This.

19
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:22,831
A family at the water's edge, arguing about dinner, at the end of an ordinary day.

20
00:01:22,921 --> 00:01:24,443
And then the reeds move.

21
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:26,054
Renn stops mid-sentence.

22
00:01:26,144 --> 00:01:27,652
Not because he's brave.

23
00:01:27,692 --> 00:01:33,910
Because he's a talker, and a talker knows exactly what silence sounds like when it isn't his.

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,383
Sena's hands go still over the fish.

25
00:01:36,423 --> 00:01:40,557
Tep looks at the reeds, then at Renn, then at the reeds again.

26
00:01:40,647 --> 00:01:44,709
Nobody says anything. The water laps. Something moves again —

27
00:01:44,749 --> 00:01:51,300
a shape, sliding between the trees on the far side, too big for a bird and too quiet for a person.

28
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:52,831
Sena picks up a stone.

29
00:01:52,871 --> 00:01:58,417
Renn picks up his spear, which he is holding by the wrong end, and does not notice.

30
00:01:58,507 --> 00:02:02,379
And then the shape steps out of the trees, and it's a deer.

31
00:02:02,469 --> 00:02:08,623
A young one. Thin, twitchy, ridiculous. It blinks at them like they've interrupted something.

32
00:02:08,713 --> 00:02:14,927
Renn exhales, says something clever, and lowers his spear. Sena shakes her head. Tep laughs —

33
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:20,306
actually laughs, out loud, the way kids do when the adults have been frightened.

34
00:02:20,396 --> 00:02:22,991
And then the deer stops looking at them.

35
00:02:23,081 --> 00:02:26,819
It's looking past them. At the reeds. Behind their backs.

36
00:02:26,909 --> 00:02:27,759
The deer runs.

37
00:02:27,849 --> 00:02:33,755
And what walks out after it is not a lion, though your brain will try to tell you it is.

38
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:38,079
It's heavier through the shoulders. Its front legs are too long.

39
00:02:38,119 --> 00:02:41,254
And when it opens its mouth, you see the teeth.

40
00:02:41,344 --> 00:02:46,626
Two of them. Long as your hand. Curving down past its jaw like a pair of blades.

41
00:02:46,716 --> 00:02:49,702
Homotherium. The sabre-toothed cat of Europe.

42
00:02:49,742 --> 00:02:57,301
A predator that stood about as tall as a person at the shoulder and weighed more than three of Renn put together.

43
00:02:57,391 --> 00:03:01,263
And it is standing between Renn's family and the path home.

44
00:03:01,353 --> 00:03:06,496
Now — everything you've seen in films says Renn should charge it. Roar at it.

45
00:03:06,536 --> 00:03:07,977
Wave the spear around.

46
00:03:08,067 --> 00:03:11,737
Renn does something far less cinematic, and far smarter.

47
00:03:11,827 --> 00:03:13,281
He doesn't move at all.

48
00:03:13,371 --> 00:03:15,752
Sena doesn't move. Tep doesn't move.

49
00:03:15,792 --> 00:03:25,031
The three of them stand there like they've been switched off, because that is what living things do when something with teeth is deciding.

50
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:28,109
The cat looks at them. Not hungry. Not angry.

51
00:03:28,149 --> 00:03:33,156
Interested, the way you're interested in a bus that might be about to move.

52
00:03:33,246 --> 00:03:39,266
Then it turns, walks down to the shoreline, and disappears along the water without a sound.

53
00:03:39,356 --> 00:03:43,686
That's it. That's the whole encounter. No fight. No heroic stand.

54
00:03:43,726 --> 00:03:51,821
Just three people standing very still until the biggest animal they have ever seen decides they're not worth the trouble.

55
00:03:51,911 --> 00:03:54,104
They wait. Ten heartbeats. Twenty.

56
00:03:54,194 --> 00:03:58,328
Then Sena lets out a breath, and Tep starts crying, and Renn —

57
00:03:58,368 --> 00:04:00,819
Renn finally looks down at his hands.

58
00:04:00,909 --> 00:04:06,676
He's been holding the spear backwards this entire time. The point has been behind him.

59
00:04:06,716 --> 00:04:08,634
Pointing at his own shoulder.

60
00:04:08,674 --> 00:04:15,657
He has been defending his family from a sabre-toothed cat with the blunt end of the only weapon he owns.

61
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:21,901
He turns it around. Quietly. Carefully. Like if he does it slowly enough, nobody will notice.

62
00:04:21,991 --> 00:04:22,774
Sena notices.

63
00:04:22,864 --> 00:04:23,580
Tep notices.

64
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:28,347
Tep stops crying, looks at Renn, and laughs so hard he has to sit down.

65
00:04:28,437 --> 00:04:35,458
They walk home in the dark, all three of them, close together, and Renn does not talk for the entire way.

66
00:04:35,498 --> 00:04:39,224
Which, honestly, is the bravest thing he does all night.

67
00:04:39,314 --> 00:04:39,476
So.

68
00:04:39,516 --> 00:04:48,557
Tomorrow night, when you're walking to your car, and you hear something behind you, and you speed up and tell yourself it was nothing —

69
00:04:48,647 --> 00:04:51,377
You'll know exactly who you got that from.

70
00:04:51,467 --> 00:04:56,206
A man by a lake. Two hundred thousand years ago. Same dark. Same sound.

71
00:04:56,246 --> 00:04:58,696
Same feeling in the back of the neck.

72
00:04:58,786 --> 00:05:01,650
The only difference is what's out there now.

73
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:08,700
And the next time you hear the reeds move, you might want to check which end of the spear you're holding.
